#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:161
msgid ""
"If you accidentally use an unsupported feature, the compiler will produce a "
"clear error: `\"CUDA-OXIDE: FORBIDDEN CRATE IN DEVICE CODE\"` with a list of "
"allowed crates (`core`, `alloc`, `cuda_device`, and your local crate)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:166
msgid "Conditional compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:168
msgid ""
"`#[cfg(...)]` works in device code exactly as it does anywhere else in Rust. "
"What is missing is anything to gate *on*: the compiler supplies no target-"
"derived `cfg`, so a kernel has no way to ask which architecture it is being "
"compiled for. Arch requirements live in doc comments and are enforced by the "
"caller, which is why an example that needs `redux.sync` checks `ctx."
"compute_capability()` on the host and skips rather than specializing the "
"kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:176
msgid ""
"`--device-cfg NAME` is how you supply one yourself. It is repeatable, and "
"each occurrence becomes a `--cfg NAME` in the build's rustflags:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:179
msgid ""
"# From the crate directory, not with an example name -- see below.\n"
"cargo oxide build --device-cfg ampere_up\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:184
msgid ""
"// One instruction where the target allows it, a shuffle tree everywhere "
"else.\n"
"#[cfg(ampere_up)]\n"
"let total = warp::redux_sync_add(u32::MAX, value);\n"
"\n"
"#[cfg(not(ampere_up))]\n"
"let total = {\n"
"    let mut acc = value;\n"
"    let mut offset = 16;\n"
"    while offset > 0 {\n"
"        acc = acc.wrapping_add(warp::shuffle_xor(acc, offset));\n"
"        offset /= 2;\n"
"    }\n"
"    acc\n"
"};\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:201
msgid "Four things are worth knowing before reaching for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:203
msgid ""
"**It is not limited to device code.** The flag travels as a rustflag, so "
"every crate cargo compiles for that build sees the `cfg`, host code included."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:206
msgid ""
"**It switches `build` into passthrough mode.** Passing it means `build` no "
"longer takes an example name, so `cargo oxide build my_example --device-cfg "
"ampere_up` is rejected; run it from the crate's own directory instead. "
"(`test` is passthrough already, flag or no flag.) If the gate can live in "
"the crate's manifest, an ordinary Cargo feature (`#[cfg(feature = "
"\"ampere_up\")]`) does the same job without giving up example-name "
"invocations; `--device-cfg` earns its keep when you need a `cfg` injected "
"without touching any Cargo.toml."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:215
msgid ""
"**rustc warns about an undeclared `cfg` name.** The `unexpected_cfgs` lint "
"checks every `#[cfg(...)]` against the declared set, and an injected `--cfg "
"ampere_up` is not in it, so each use prints an `unexpected cfg condition "
"name` warning. Declare it in the kernel crate's manifest to silence them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:221
msgid ""
"[lints.rust]\n"
"unexpected_cfgs = { level = \"warn\", check-cfg = [\"cfg(ampere_up)\"] }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:226
msgid ""
"**Nothing ties it to `--arch`.** If the `cfg` name stands for an "
"architecture, you are the one keeping the two in step -- passing `--device-"
"cfg ampere_up` without the matching `--arch` will happily build the "
"specialized path for whatever target was selected, and PTX that the "
"assembler then rejects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:233
msgid "Loop unrolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:235
msgid ""
"Inside a `#[kernel]` or `#[device]` function, put `#[unroll]` directly on a "
"loop whose trip count is known at compile time. This requests that the "
"compiler remove the loop and lay out copies of its body:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:239
msgid ""
"#[kernel]\n"
"pub fn sum_four(mut out: DisjointSlice<u32>) {\n"
"    let tid = thread::index_1d();\n"
"    if let Some(out_elem) = out.get_mut(tid) {\n"
"        let mut sum = 0;\n"
"        let mut i = 0;\n"
"        #[unroll]\n"
"        while i < 4 {\n"
"            sum += i;\n"
"            i += 1;\n"
"        }\n"
"        *out_elem = sum;\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:256
msgid ""
"The pass currently recognizes explicit counted `while` loops. Range-based "
"`for` loops are not yet recognized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:259
msgid ""
"Use `#[unroll(N)]`, where `N >= 2`, when the trip count is only known at "
"runtime. The loop then does `N` iterations' work per trip. A small remainder "
"loop handles any leftover iterations, so `n` does not have to be divisible "
"by `N`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:263
msgid ""
"let mut i = 0;\n"
"#[unroll(4)]\n"
"while i < n {\n"
"    process(i);\n"
"    i += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:272
msgid ""
"An annotated loop may contain other loops. Only the loop carrying the "
"annotation is unrolled; each inner loop is copied intact and remains a loop. "
"Add a separate annotation to an inner loop if you want to unroll it too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:276
msgid ""
"Loops with several `continue` paths are supported. Full `#[unroll]` also "
"preserves `break` paths and loops with more than one exit target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:279
msgid ""
"Partial `#[unroll(N)]` currently requires the loop condition to be the only "
"exit. If the loop has a `break` or another exit, the compiler warns and does "
"not unroll that loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:283
msgid ""
"Partial unrolling also requires a counted-up loop: the counter must have a "
"positive step, use `<` or `<=`, and compare against a limit that does not "
"change inside the loop. The compiler warns and does not unroll unsupported "
"requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:287
msgid ""
"To keep generated code bounded, one annotation may create at most 1,024 body "
"copies, 8,192 cloned basic blocks, and 65,536 cloned operations. A larger "
"request warns and is not unrolled. Full variable-debug builds also skip "
"unrolling because they keep loop variables in memory instead of SSA form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:292
msgid ""
"Unrolling trades larger generated code for fewer branches and more "
"optimization opportunities. Use it for small or performance-critical loops, "
"and measure the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:297
msgid ""
"For how the compiler analyzes and rewrites annotated loops, including the "
"stage-index peephole, see [Compiler Optimizations](../compiler/compiler-"
"optimizations.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:301
msgid "`#[launch_bounds]` -- occupancy hints"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:303
msgid ""
"The `#[launch_bounds]` attribute tells the compiler how many threads you "
"intend to launch per block. This lets the PTX assembler make better register "
"allocation decisions and can improve occupancy:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:307
msgid ""
"#[kernel]\n"
"#[launch_bounds(256, 2)]\n"
"pub fn optimized_kernel(mut out: DisjointSlice<f32>) {\n"
"    // ...\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

