#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:322
msgid ".entry optimized_kernel .maxntid 256, 1, 1 .minnctapersm 2 { ... }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:326
msgid ""
"`.maxntid` bounds the product `x * y * z`, so a 256-thread bound admits "
"`(256, 1, 1)`, `(16, 16, 1)` and `(4, 8, 8)` alike. Add "
"`#[launch_contract(block = (x, y, z))]` when one exact shape is required. "
"That emits `.reqntid` in place of `.maxntid`, which the driver enforces per "
"axis:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:331
msgid ".entry exact_kernel .reqntid 256, 1, 1 .minnctapersm 2 { ... }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:335
msgid ""
"The two directives are mutually exclusive; ptxas rejects an entry declaring "
"both, so a contracted kernel emits `.reqntid` alone. `.minnctapersm` is an "
"occupancy hint and composes with either."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:340
msgid "`#[launch_bounds]` must appear **after** `#[kernel]`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:342
msgid ""
"#[kernel]\n"
"#[launch_bounds(256, 2)]   // correct\n"
"pub fn my_kernel(...) { }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:350
msgid "When the bound forces spills"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:352
msgid ""
"A launch bound is ultimately a cap on registers per thread: promising ptxas "
"more resident threads leaves each of them fewer registers to work with. If "
"the kernel needs more than the cap allows, ptxas does not fail -- it "
"**spills** the excess to local memory, and the occupancy the bound bought is "
"then paid for again on every access to a spilled value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:358
msgid ""
"That is easy to miss, because the build succeeds. cuda-oxide warns instead, "
"at the kernel's definition span:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:361
msgid ""
"warning: kernel `cuda_oxide_kernel_a1b2c3d4_my_kernel` compiled with\n"
"         `#[launch_bounds(256, 2)]` and spills registers\n"
"  = note: ptxas reports 96 bytes spill stores and 96 bytes spill loads\n"
"  = note: ptxas allocated 40 registers per thread\n"
"  = help: relax `min_blocks_per_sm` or reduce register pressure\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:369
msgid ""
"(Byte counts and register totals are whatever ptxas reported for your "
"kernel.) Only kernels carrying `#[launch_bounds]` are checked -- without a "
"bound there is no promise for ptxas to satisfy at the expense of registers. "
"The warning can only fire in builds that materialize a native cubin (`--"
"materialize-cubin`, or any build that embeds a cubin): a plain PTX build "
"never runs ptxas, so there is no resource report to inspect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:376
msgid ""
"The usual responses are to raise `max_threads`, drop or relax `min_blocks`, "
"or cut register pressure in the kernel itself. Spilling is not automatically "
"wrong: a kernel can be faster spilling a little at high occupancy than not "
"spilling at low occupancy. The warning exists because that is a trade worth "
"making deliberately rather than by accident."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:383
msgid ""
"`#[allow(...)]` does **not** silence these. They are raw span diagnostics "
"rather than lints, so the suppression attribute does not apply to them. The "
"escape hatch is an environment variable, meant for builds that measured the "
"spill and accepted it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:388
msgid ""
"CUDA_OXIDE_NO_SPILL_WARN=1 cargo oxide build my_kernels --materialize-cubin\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:394
msgid "The collector -- how device code is discovered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:396
msgid ""
"When you build with `cargo oxide`, the `rustc-codegen-cuda` backend runs a "
"**collector** pass that determines which functions to compile for the GPU:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:399
msgid ""
"Scan all compilation units for functions in the reserved "
"`cuda_oxide_kernel_<hash>_` namespace (generated by `#[kernel]`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:401
msgid ""
"For each kernel, **traverse the call graph** and collect all transitively "
"reachable functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:403
msgid "**Filter** each callee against the allowed-crate list:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-programming/kernels-and-device-functions.md:382
